    16	
    17	For EVERY polynomial H in k[x,y], restrict to z=H(x,y). No affine-linear
    18	map k^3->k^2 applied to these three restricted outputs has nonzero constant
    19	Jacobian in (x,y). This includes every constant z-slice, every polynomial
    20	graph degree, and every rank-two linear output projection. Precomposing the
    21	graph parametrization by a polynomial automorphism of the plane does not
    22	change this conclusion.
    23	
    24	No statement about other graph orientations, general embedded planes,
    25	nonlinear three-output projections, rational graphs, or arbitrary 3D maps
    26	is made. In particular x=0 gives (P,Q)=(z+4y^2,y), a plane automorphism;
    27	it is an explicit negative control against extending the theorem to all
    28	graph orientations.
    29	
    30	## Degree-uniform proof
    31	
    32	Write d=deg H when H is nonconstant, and d=0 when H is constant, including
    33	H=0. In the nonconstant case let h be its nonzero degree-d homogeneous
    34	part and put T=x^3 h. In the constant case H=c put
    35	T=x^2(cx+3y). Then T is nonzero homogeneous of degree d+3 and
    36	T_x is nonzero of degree d+2. For d>0, T is divisible by x^3, so T_x=0
    37	would contradict characteristic zero; for d=0,
    38	T_x=3cx^2+6xy is visibly nonzero, including c=0.
    39	
    40	The leading total-degree forms of the three restricted outputs are
    41	
    42	```
    43	p=y^3 T,       q=3y^2 T,       r=-T,
    44	```
    45	
    46	of respective degrees d+6,d+5,d+3. For d>0 the z-terms dominate
    47	the z-free terms; for d=0 their two same-degree contributions combine
    48	into the displayed T, so the constant case is not discarded.
    49	
    50	Direct differentiation gives
